 # Classical Ray Tracing Integration
 
-Also called: Analytical Volume Integration for Homogeneous Spheres, Ray Marching Traceray, Ray Tracing Integration for 3DGS, Ray casting with geometry, Ray tracing volume rendering, Ray-Sphere Volume Rendering with Absorption and Uniform Scattering, SciVis Shading
+Also called: Kajiya and Von Herzen
 
 ## Overview
-Classical Ray Tracing Integration is a volume rendering technique that combines analytical geometric intersection with ray marching to compute pixel color by combining background transmission (attenuated by absorption) and uniform in-scattering [S1]. It solves the problem of rendering volumetric objects with defined boundaries (such as spheres) or integrating geometric visibility into volume pipelines, offering a closed-form solution for homogeneous volumes or a deferred shading heuristic for complex scalar fields [S1, S2]. Unlike standard direct volume ray casting which samples uniformly along the entire ray, this method explicitly calculates entry and exit points to limit sampling to the volume interior and applies Beer's law for transmission [S1]. It is distinguished from [Alpha Compositing Ray Casting](922-alpha-compositing-ray-casting.md) by its handling of homogeneous spheres via analytical integration, and from [Single Scattering Volume Rendering with Ray Marching](1532-single-scattering-volume-rendering-with-ray-marching.md) by its assumption of uniform scattering color [S1].
+Classical Ray Tracing Integration is a volume rendering technique that computes pixel color by combining background transmission (attenuated by absorption) and uniform in-scattering [S1]. It solves the problem of rendering volumetric objects with defined boundaries or integrating geometric visibility directly into volume pipelines, offering a closed-form solution for homogeneous volumes or a deferred shading heuristic for complex scalar fields [S1, S2]. Unlike standard direct volume ray casting which samples uniformly along the entire ray, this method explicitly calculates entry and exit points to limit sampling to the volume interior and applies Beer's law for transmission [S1]. It is distinguished from [Alpha Compositing Ray Casting](922-alpha-compositing-ray-casting.md) by its handling of homogeneous spheres via analytical integration, and from [Single Scattering Volume Rendering with Ray Marching](1532-single-scattering-volume-rendering-with-ray-marching.md) by its assumption of uniform scattering color [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in two primary modes: analytical integration for homogeneous spheres and ray marching with shading heuristics for general volume data. Both modes rely on the volume rendering integral discretized into a summation over sample points along a ray [S5].
