 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of samples along the ray. For analytical spheres, it is $O(1)$ per pixel. For marching, it depends on volume density and step size.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ for analytical mode. $O(V)$ for volume data storage in marching mode.
-*   **Performance**: Analytical integration is extremely fast and exact for homogeneous spheres. Ray marching is slower but handles complex geometries. Early ray termination optimizes performance in dense regions [S2, S3].
-*   **Strengths**: Exact solution for homogeneous volumes; integrates geometric visibility; supports shading heuristics for complex data.
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of samples along the ray [G]. For analytical spheres, it is $O(1)$ per pixel [S1]. For marching, it depends on volume density and step size [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ for analytical mode. $O(V)$ for volume data storage in marching mode [G].
+*   **Performance**: Analytical integration is extremely fast and exact for homogeneous spheres [S1]. Ray marching is slower but handles complex geometries [S2]. Early ray termination optimizes performance in dense regions [S2, S3].
+*   **Strengths**: Exact solution for homogeneous volumes; integrates geometric visibility; supports shading heuristics for complex data [S1, S2, S3].
 *   **Limitations**: Analytical mode is limited to simple shapes (spheres); uniform scattering assumption is unrealistic for many volumes; heuristic shading may produce artifacts if the highest density point is not representative [S1, S2].
 *   **When to use**: Ideal for rendering simple volumetric objects (e.g., clouds, smoke balls) or when integrating geometric surfaces with volume data [S1, S3]. Avoid for highly anisotropic or spatially varying scattering volumes [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from early volume rendering techniques that combined ray tracing with volume data [S1]. It was formalized in the context of scientific visualization shading, where analytical integration for homogeneous spheres provided a baseline for more complex methods [S1]. The integration of classical ray tracing into volume rendering pipelines was proposed to handle visibility and geometric merging, such as superimposing radiation treatment planning isodose surfaces over CT data [S3]. The heuristic shading approach was developed to balance performance and visual quality in scientific visualization [S2]. Recent applications include integrating ray tracing for interreflections in 3D Gaussian Splatting [S4].
